  "mechanisms": [
    "The initial hypothesis was that high population density acts as an aversive stimulus, raising drive level and affecting performance (facilitating simple tasks, interfering with complex tasks). However, the null findings suggest that density per se, under the controlled conditions of the experiments, does not function as an ordinary aversive, arousal-inducing stimulus.",
    "The authors speculate that any effects of high density might be more complicated, primarily affecting social behaviors and interactions among people, rather than individual task performance."
  ],
  "moderators": [
    "Group size (5 vs. 9 subjects)",
    "Sex of participants",
    "Task type (simple vs. complex/creative)",
    "Motivation level (bonus vs. no bonus)",
    "Temporal factors (first vs. second task presentation, performance across days, duration of exposure)"
  ],
  "implementation_implications": [
    "Density per se should not be considered inherently evil; if logistical problems (e.g., lack of air, noise, physical discomfort) can be solved, high density might not have negative effects on people and could even be encouraged or allowed in certain contexts.",
    "The research suggests that concerns about urban concentrations and their negative effects should focus on logistical and organizational problems rather than density itself as an inherent social evil."
  ],
